      "matchText": "Tullumayo",
      "term": "Tullumayo",
      "definition": "One of the two small rivers that bracketed the Inca-era core of Cuzco, running along its eastern edge. Together with the Saphi to the west, it framed the body of the puma whose shape the redesigned imperial capital was meant to trace."
    },
    {
      "matchText": "Saphi",
      "term": "Saphi",
      "definition": "The river along the western edge of imperial Cuzco's core, paired with the Tullumayo on the east. The two waterways defined the flanks of the puma-shaped city plan and were later largely channeled underground beneath the colonial streets."
    },
    {
      "matchText": "Pumachupan",
      "term": "Pumachupan",
      "definition": "Quechua for \"puma's tail\" — the wedge of land southeast of Cuzco where the Tullumayo and Saphi rivers met, forming the tail of the puma whose silhouette the redesigned capital was built to resemble."
    },
    {
      "matchText": "Hurin Cuzco",
      "term": "Hurin Cuzco",
      "definition": "\"Lower Cuzco,\" the southern half of the imperial capital under the Inca dual-division (moiety) system, paired with and ritually opposed to Hanan (upper) Cuzco. Each half housed the lineage corporations of different past rulers and competed with the other for honor in state festivals."
    },
