    {
      "matchText": "Antis",
      "term": "Antis",
      "definition": "The Anti were the forest peoples of the eastern Andean foothills and upper Amazon, on the Inca empire's humid eastern frontier. The Inca named their eastern quadrant, Antisuyu, after them, but never seriously controlled the lowland rainforest beyond the foothills."
    },
    {
      "matchText": "Cayambi",
      "term": "Cayambi",
      "definition": "The Cayambi were one of the small highland kingdoms of northern Ecuador, allied with the neighboring Caranqui in a long confederated resistance to Inca conquest. Their war against Huayna Capac dragged on for roughly two decades before the north was finally pacified around 1515."
    },
    {
      "matchText": "Ibarra",
      "term": "Ibarra",
      "definition": "Ibarra is a city in the northern Ecuadorian highlands, near the lake the Inca called Yahuarcocha (\"lake of blood\") after the Caranqui defeat fought beside it around 1515."
    },
    {
      "matchText": "Ancasmayo River",
      "term": "Ancasmayo River",
      "definition": "The Ancasmayo (Angasmayo) was the river the Inca treated as the northern limit of their empire, on what is now the Colombia-Ecuador border. It marked the empire's maximum reach to the north under Huayna Capac."
    },
    {
      "matchText": "Sun Temple",
      "term": "Sun Temple",
      "definition": "Each major Inca center had a temple dedicated to Inti, the sun god, modeled on the great Coricancha at Cuzco. Huayna Capac's northern capital at Tumipampa was laid out with its own Sun Temple as a deliberate copy of the imperial template."
    }
  ],
